from ./model import SemanticModel
from ./model/OpenItems import OpenItems
from ./measures import Measures
from ./measures/Readiness import Readiness
from ./instructions import Instructions
from ./meaning/Provenance import Provenance

export anchor Output:
    description:
        Analysis builds one semantic model, and everything it shows is a view
        of that model. A view chooses what to show; a format chooses how to
        write it. Any view can be written in any format.

    views:
        ```text
        overview       counts, measures and readiness for the whole scope,
                       and the open decisions to settle first     (the default)
        facts          every fact, with its state, strength, conditions and source
        graph          every thing and every link between things
        open           every open item
        gaps           only gaps
        ambiguities    only ambiguities
        contradictions only contradictions
        unrecognized   only unrecognized statements
        measures       every measure for every entity, with the counts behind it
        readiness      which entities are implementation-ready, and why not
        instructions   an instruction document for every entity
        trace          everything that came from one statement, or everything
                       one fact came from
        ```

        The model is described in @{SemanticModel}, open items in
        @{OpenItems}, measures in @{Measures}, readiness in @{Readiness},
        instructions in @{Instructions}, and tracing in @{Provenance}.

    scope:
        A view can be narrowed to part of the model: an entity, such as
        Button; a part, such as Button.disabled; a statement, such as
        Button.disabled/s3; or a fact or open item, by id. Narrowing a view
        never changes the model it is cut from. Button's facts are the same
        whether the whole project is shown or only Button.

    settleFirst:
        The overview lists the open decisions to settle first. Contradictions
        come first, because they block building altogether; then unrecognized
        statements, because they may hide anything; then gaps; then
        ambiguities. Within each kind, an open item comes earlier the more
        entities depend on the entity it is about, and otherwise in source
        order.

    formats:
        ```text
        text       for reading in a terminal; the default
        markdown   for pull requests and documentation
        json       the model itself, for other tools
        html       a single self-contained page, which loads nothing from the
                   network, to open in a browser or share
        ```

        In json, facts, conditions, statements and open items are listed
        once, in source order, with their ids, and everything else refers to
        them by id. The shape of each is given by the Fact, Condition,
        SourceRecord and OpenItem anchors.

    destination:
        A view is written to standard output unless an out file is named.
        Progress and diagnostics never go to standard output, so a view can
        always be redirected to a file intact.
